=================
File Permissions
================

ubuntu => /home/ubuntu ==> f1.txt

rakesh  => /home/rakesh  ==> f1.txt

rajesh  => /home/rajesh  ==> f2.txt

teja => /home/teja ==> f3.txt

=> We can create several user accounts in a single Linux VM

=> Multiple users can connect to a single Linux VM at the same time

=> Different users can access different files based on permissions

=> To protect files and directories we use file permissions

=> In Linux, file permissions are divided into 3 types

```
    r => read
    w => write
    x => execute
```

=> A file/directory contains 3 permission sections

```
    user (owner) => u

    group         => g

    others        => o
```

=> Example:

```
    rwxrwxrwx f1.txt

    user:
    read + write + execute

    group:
    read + write + execute

    others:
    read + write + execute


    rw-r--r-- f2.txt

    user:
    read + write

    group:
    read

    others:
    read


    rwxr-xr-x java

    user:
    read + write + execute

    group:
    read + execute

    others:
    read + execute


    r-xr----x sbms

    user:
    read + execute

    group:
    read

    others:
    execute
```

=> To change file permissions we use `chmod` command

# Giving execute permission for user

chmod u+x f1.txt

# Giving write permission for group

chmod g+w f1.txt

# Remove execute permission for others

chmod o-x f1.txt

# Remove all permissions for others

chmod o-rwx f1.txt

# Give all permissions for group

chmod g+rwx f1.txt

====================================
File Permissions in Numeric Format
==================================

0 => No Permission

1 => Execute

2 => Write

3 => Write + Execute

4 => Read

5 => Read + Execute

6 => Read + Write

7 => Read + Write + Execute

chmod 765 f1.txt

```
    user:
    read + write + execute

    group:
    read + write

    others:
    read + execute
```

chmod 456 f1.txt

```
    user:
    read

    group:
    read + execute

    others:
    read + write
```

